T*
/F1 14 Tf
(4. Chunking Strategy) Tj
T*
/F1 11 Tf
(The chunking strategy uses 500 character chunks with an overlap of 100 characters. A) Tj
T*
/F1 11 Tf
(chunk size of 500 characters keeps enough context for a meaningful passage while staying) Tj
T*
/F1 11 Tf
(small enough that the embedding is not diluted by unrelated text.) Tj
T*
/F1 11 Tf
(The overlap of 100 characters is roughly one fifth of a chunk. This prevents a fact that) Tj
T*
/F1 11 Tf
(falls exactly on a boundary from being split across two chunks, where neither half would) Tj
T*
/F1 11 Tf
(retrieve it well.) Tj
T*
T*
T*
/F1 14 Tf
(5. Vector Database) Tj
T*
/F1 11 Tf
(The vector database used for storage is ChromaDB. It persists an HNSW index on disk,) Tj
T*
/F1 11 Tf
(which makes cosine similarity search fast even as the collection grows, and it keeps the) Tj
T*
/F1 11 Tf
(collection metadata alongside each vector so that citations can be resolved.) Tj
T*
/F1 11 Tf
(Each vector carries metadata recording the source file name, the chunk number and the) Tj
T*
/F1 11 Tf
(page number. This is what makes it possible to show the user exactly which passage) Tj
T*
/F1 11 Tf
(produced an answer.) Tj
T*
T*
T*
/F1 14 Tf
(6. Retrieval) Tj
T*
/F1 11 Tf
(Retrieval works by embedding the question with the same model used for the documents,) Tj
T*
/F1 11 Tf
(then performing a similarity search that returns the top 5 chunks ranked by cosine) Tj
T*
/F1 11 Tf
(similarity.) Tj
T*
/F1 11 Tf
(A similarity score close to 1 means the chunk means almost the same thing as the) Tj
T*
/F1 11 Tf
(question, while a score near 0 means the two are unrelated. The interface displays these) Tj
T*
/F1 11 Tf
(scores so retrieval quality can be inspected directly.) Tj
T*
T*
T*
/F1 14 Tf
(7. Answer Generation) Tj
T*
/F1 11 Tf
(Answer generation sends the retrieved context and the question to a large language) Tj
T*
/F1 11 Tf
(model. The prompt instructs the model to answer only from the provided context and to) Tj
T*
/F1 11 Tf
(reply that the documents do not contain enough information when the context is) Tj
T*
/F1 11 Tf
(insufficient.) Tj
T*
/F1 11 Tf
(Because generation is conditioned on retrieved text, the system cannot invent facts) Tj
T*
/F1 11 Tf
(about documents it has never seen. The cost of this design is that answer quality is) Tj
T*
/F1 11 Tf
(bounded by retrieval quality.) Tj
T*
T*
T*
/F1 14 Tf
(8. Advantages of RAG) Tj
T*
/F1 11 Tf
(The advantages of RAG include grounded answers that can be traced to a source, knowledge) Tj
T*
/F1 11 Tf
(that can be updated by editing documents rather than retraining a model, and) Tj
T*
/F1 11 Tf
(transparency because the retrieved passages can be shown to the user.) Tj
T*
/F1 11 Tf
(RAG also reduces hallucination compared with asking a model directly, since the model is) Tj
T*
/F1 11 Tf
(instructed to rely on the supplied context.) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
